ready: 1
kind: module
id: trust
name: Trust
summary: >-
  Decides how much each source of memory is trusted: the trust tier of every capture and
  derived memory, the input gate that holds behavior-changing memories with weak support, and
  the agent's trust assessments of other entities. Runs in the Kernle core inside the daemon
  and the Python package.
runs_in:
  - sys.daemon
  - sys.python
owns:
  - Source trust tiers (RULE-TRUST-TIER)
  - The input gate (RULE-TRUST-GATE)
  - Entity trust assessments (DATA-TRUST-ASSESSMENT)
invariants:
  - A derived memory is never trusted more than the weakest source it depends on.
  - A behavior-changing memory supported only by outside content never applies without review.
  - Owner-authored and protected memories never change automatically.
rationale: >-
  Persistent prompt injection works by getting an agent to remember an instruction it read
  somewhere. One module decides trust so every write path (explicit calls, transcript capture,
  processing, sleep, imports, sync) applies the same tiers and the same gate, instead of each
  path inventing its own notion of "safe".


  Entity trust lives here too because the appraisal label "distrust" and the gate both need to
  know which sources have misled the agent before.
